const PartnerFaq=[{q:"How can I support her when she’s feeling emotional or overwhelmed?",a:"Not every feeling needs fixing. Listen first, without interrupting or jumping to solutions — and don’t assume it’s “just hormones.” Ask what she actually needs: comfort, reassurance, practical help, or space. Try: “Do you want me to just listen, or help you figure this out?”"},{q:"What’s the difference between validating her feelings and agreeing with her?",a:"Validating means taking her emotional experience seriously — not necessarily agreeing with every detail. You can say “That sounds really frustrating” without ruling on who was right. It tells her she’s heard, not that you’ve taken her side."},{q:"What can I do when she’s feeling more sensitive or needs reassurance?",a:"Offer affection if she’s open to it, stay present, and follow through on what you say you’ll do — reliability reads as love. Ask rather than assume: “What would help right now?” Sensitivity isn’t a reason to dismiss what she’s telling you."},{q:"How can I support her during her period?",a:"Small, concrete things land best: a warm drink, taking a task off her plate, a heating pad or a massage. Respect that her energy may be lower, and offer closeness or space — whichever she actually wants. Little Things She Loves is a good place to check first."},{q:"How do hormonal changes affect mood, energy, and desire?",a:"Hormones shift through the cycle and can genuinely affect energy, mood, and desire for some women — but they don’t write the whole story. They don’t determine her personality, decide whether her concerns are valid, or explain away how she feels. Treat the cycle as context, not a verdict."},{q:"How can I maintain intimacy and connection throughout her cycle?",a:"Intimacy isn’t just physical — touch, quality time, real conversation, and a bit of playfulness all count. Desire and consent matter every day, not just some days, so follow her lead rather than the calendar. Different weeks may call for different kinds of closeness, and that’s normal."},{q:"What should I do when we have a disagreement?",a:"Listen to understand her side before defending yours, then share your perspective without contempt, raised voices, or shutting down. Aim for repair, not winning. This one goes both ways — healthy communication isn’t a chore assigned to just one of you."}];
